# An Americium-Containing Metal-Organic Framework: A Platform for Studying Transplutonium Elements

## abstract
The synthesis, structure, and spectroscopic characterization of the first transplutonium metal-organic framework (MOF) is described. The preparation and structure of Am-GWMOF-6, [Am2 (C6 H8 O4 )3 (H2 O)2 ][(C10 H8 N2 )], is analogous to that of the isostructural trivalent lanthanide-only containing material GWMOF-6. The presented MOF architecture is used as a platform to probe Am3+ coordination chemistry and guest-enhanced luminescent emission, whereas the framework itself provides a means to monitor the effects of self-irradiation upon crystallinity over time. Presented here is a discussion of these properties and the opportunities that MOFs provide in the structural and spectroscopic study of actinides.

The minor actinide elements americium and curium have been the subject of extensive study for decades[1] and renewed interest in recent years has focused both upon the fundamental understanding of their coordination chemistry as well as applications in improving separations processes. These synthetic elements, many with long-lived isotopes, are all generated through cycles of neutron bombardment and beta-decay of actinides in nuclear fuel in reactors and pose unique and fundamental scientific challenges. As such, there are many active areas of inquiry in the transplutonium field, from studies of covalency in 5f elements[2] to applications and exploration of new systems for the difficult task of chemically separating $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ from trivalent lanthanide fission products in spent nuclear fuel for the reduction of long-term radiological hazards.[3]
Many previous studies have highlighted the challenges presented by similar coordination behavior between $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ and the trivalent lanthanides while attempting to exploit subtle differences in ionic radii to better understand bonding in 4f and 5f elements in separations chemistry.[4] Whereas the similarities in chemical and ionic radius between trivalent lanthanides and actinides are a challenge for separations, they consequently provide opportunities to utilize established lanthanide based materials for the incorporation of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ ; especially so in the realm of metal–organic hybrid materials. Further, the compendium of f-element containing hybrid materials is vast[5] and the materials themselves provide abundant opportunities for targeting desired structural motifs and properties. One such approach is to employ trivalent actinides $(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{n}^{3+})$ metal centers as nodes and dicarboxylate ligands as linkers in the formation of MOFs.
The term “metal–organic frameworks” has been used to describe an expansive array of materials with incredibly diverse properties and applications in gas sorption and separations,[6] catalysis,[7] nanotechnology,[8] and biomedicine,[9] as well as potential applications relevant to the nuclear fuel cycle, such as for Xe capture[10] and uranium extraction from seawater,[11] among others.[12] Many of these applications harness the fundamental properties of a MOF architecture, e.g. a rigid yet porous structures that can be used as a stable scaffold for capture, storage, catalytic reaction, or gradual release of targeted molecules. Alternatively, and perhaps more relevant to work in our group, MOFs can be designed for the inclusion of aromatic molecules, as part of the framework or in the pores, for probing spectroscopic signatures or exploring novel electronic applications of the metal nodes themselves through energy transfer, semiconductivity, or photocatalytic activity.[7a,13] We have therefore chosen to utilize a MOF as a structurally robust platform for studying the coordination chemistry and electronic structure of transplutonium elements.
With respect to the criteria above, the GWMOF-6 architecture, originally synthesized by de Lill and Cahill,[14] is a prime target for this actinide only effort as this structure exhibits a rigid framework with guest aromatic molecules contributing to enhanced emission properties. Further, the synthesis of GWMOF-6 analogues produced single-crystalline material in high yield that utilized $\mathrm{Pr}^{3+}$ $(1.\overset{\cdot}{1}26\overset{\circ}{\mathbf{A}})$ and $\mathbf{Nd}^{3+}\left(1.109\mathring{\mathbf{A}}\right)$ ,[15] which have similar ionic radii and coordination numbers to that of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}\left(1.09\mathring\mathrm{\mathbf{A}}\right)$ ,[3d] and proved to be scalable without negatively affecting the generation of large, diffraction-quality single crystals. As such, we applied the GWMOF-6 synthesis parameters[14] to the preparation of the first transplutonium MOF (Am-GWMOF-6, compound 1, $\mathrm{\small{[Am_{2}(C_{6}H_{8}O_{4})_{3}(H_{2}O)_{2}]{\cdot(C_{10}H_{8}N_{2}))}}}$ .
The GWMOF-6 platform provides a unique opportunity to explore many of the unstable, short half-life, minor actinide elements by incorporating them into a MOF architecture for structural and spectroscopic study. Owing to the aforementioned similarities in ionic radii, this MOF allows one to bypass some otherwise exploratory efforts in that an isostructural (with $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ) compound is a likely outcome. Such a “preemptive search” for a framework material is imperative considering the scarcity and expense of $^{243}\mathrm{Am}$ precursors, as well the need to limit high levels of radiation from the $^{243}\mathrm{Am}$ $(t_{1/2}=7.38\times10^{3}$ years, $0.2\mathrm{Cig^{-1}}\dot{,}$ . Accessibility and safety factors have limited the amount and scope of work done with these trivalent actinides, especially so for solid state materials of sufficient size and quantity for X-ray structural characterization, and a recent study of an organometallic Am complex ${\bf\langle A m(C_{5}M e_{4}H)_{3},}^{\prime}$ ) highlights this fact well by providing a list of all 33 CSD entries.[4e]
Compound 1, Am-GWMOF-6 $\begin{array}{r}{([\mathbf{Am}_{2}(\mathbf{C}_{6}\mathbf{H}_{8}\mathbf{O}_{4})_{3}(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}].}\end{array}$ $\mathrm{(C_{10}H_{8}N_{2})}\rangle$ ) crystallizes in the orthorhombic space group $P b c n$ and is isostructural with other $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ GWMOF-6 materials ( $\overset{\cdot}{a}=$ 21.9131(11) $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ , b = 7.7134(3) $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ , $c=19.5866(10)\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , $V=$ $3310.6(3)\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})$ . Each $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ metal center $(\mathrm{CN}=9)$ ) is coordinated by six carboxylates, from two unique adipate ligands, and a water molecule (OW1) to yield a distorted capped square antiprismatic coordination geometry (Figure 1).
$\mathbf{AmO}_{9}$ polyhedra are bridged by two bidentate carboxylates in the [010] direction to form a 1D chain and further assembled through four other adipates in the [100] and [001] directions to form the neutral three-dimensional framework (Figure 1). A neutral $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine moiety is present in the lattice, which hydrogen bonds to the framework through the coordinated water molecule. The most direct and commonly utilized lanthanide analogue for structural comparison to $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ is $\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}$ (compound 2),[16] provided herein at a comparable refinement temperature. Of note is that the structural refinement of 2 for this study revealed a unit cell with a triple a cell parameter compared to 1 and to the original GWMOF-6 report: $(a=65.6726(17)\mathrm{\AA}$ , $b=7.7371\cdot$ (2) $\mathbf{\bar{A}}$ , $c=19.5473(4)\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , $V{=}9932.3(4)\mathring{\mathrm{A}}{}^{3}\mathrm{\cdot}$ . This is likely a consequence of using a more intense X-ray source and more sensitive detector since the original GWMOF-6 report in 2005. As such, a thorough, direct comparison of selected trivalent metal–oxygen bond distances (Table S5 and S6) between compounds 1 and 2 is difficult owing to differences in asymmetric units, yet a simple look at the metal center to bound water molecule bond distances shows equivalent values within statistical error $(\mathrm{Am-O};2.469(5)\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , Avg Nd $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{O}\colon2.471\mathring{\mathbf{A}}_{\cdot}^{\setminus}}\end{array}$ . A thorough treatment of this unit cell determination and context with respect to related phases is given in Section III of the Supporting Information (SI).
Due to the high activity of the $^{243}\mathrm{Am}$ isotope, damage to the materials by self-irradiation, caused by the alpha-decay of $^{243}\mathrm{Am}$ and the gamma associated with the $^{239}\mathrm{Np}$ daughter 1 $\stackrel{\cdot}{t}_{1/2}=2.35$ days, $\gamma=278\mathrm{keV},$ , impacts significantly the longterm stability of solid-state compounds bearing these isotopes. For example, a study of an $^{243}\mathrm{Am}$ iodate $\left(\mathrm{Am}(\mathrm{IO}_{3})_{3}\right)$ monitored unit cell parameters as a measure of crystal decay and observed color changes as well as cell volume increases corresponding to a swelling of the iodate layers and perhaps molecular iodine formation.[16c] Damage from self-irradiation is certainly unsurprising, and very common in materials containing high activity actinides. For example, effects observed in $\mathrm{Pu}$ and $\mathrm{PuO}_{2}$ , where damage is associated with the alpha decay and cooresponding recoiling daughter nucleus,[17] present a unique challenge in the study of these materials.[4g,18]
Compound 1 provided an opportunity to explore the structural integrity of a rigid framework containing a transplutonium isotope. We analyzed the same single crystal used in the structural analysis of 1 over the course of several months to semi-quantitatively monitor any effect radiation damage may have on the material by comparing the unit cell dimensions and cell volume with several short collections. The initial diffraction data presented in this work was collected 26 days after the synthesis of the material, followed by data collections over the next three months (Table S4 in the SI). An expansion of the unit cell volume by approximately $2\%$ was observed between one month and two months followed by a volume slight decrease over the next month. A noticeable drop in diffraction intensity and quality proceeded over the course of those three months as well, as evidenced by increasingly poor integration statistics ( $[R_{\mathrm{int}}$ , Table S4 in the SI), demonstrating an unsurprising, yet slow loss of crystallinity over time. The structural lifetime of this single crystal is notable, though, as reasonable structural solution and refinement was achieved in all cases, indicating that the rigidity and stability of the framework could prove useful when studying nominally unstable minor actinides hybrid materials.
Beyond the similarities in the synthesis and structure, the sensitized luminescent emission observed within the ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ variants of GWMOF- $\cdot6^{[14,19]}$ highlights another rationale for the substitution of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ for $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ . Sensitized luminescent emission by utilizing the antenna effect is a well-known phenomenon within lanthanide materials[20] and has been observed in $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{n}^{3+}$ materials as well.[21] A single crystal of compound 1 was excited at multiple wavelengths associated with the absorption band of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine (Figure S13 and Figure S16 in the SI) in a range to capture the most intense emission from $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ , observed at $683\mathrm{nm}$ and $726\mathrm{nm}$ and corresponding to the ${}^{5}\mathbf{D}_{1{'}}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathbf{F}_{1{'}}$ and $^5{\mathrm{L}}_{6^{\prime}}\rightarrow^{7}{\mathrm{F}}_{2^{\prime}}$ transitions, respectively (Figure 2). The broad emission band beginning at $435\mathrm{nm}$ and extending out to $\approx600\:\mathrm{nm}$ is likely residual luminescence from the $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine due to inefficient energy transfer from the bipyridine to the $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ metal center.
Despite limited examples of spectroscopic analyses of solid-state $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ containing compounds, and unlike materials containing 4f elements, the position of absorption and emission bands are known to be highly dependent on the coordination environment around the metal center.[4d,22] This coordination environment dependency often manifests as a red-shift in the absorption spectrum or as variance in the position of the ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{1^{\prime}}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{1^{\prime}}$ and $^5{\mathrm{L}}_{6^{\prime}}\rightarrow^{7}{\mathrm{F}}_{2^{\prime}}$ transitions on the order of $10{-}15\mathrm{nm}$ , where examples range from $689\mathrm{nm}$ for ${}^{5}\mathbf{D}_{1^{'}}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathbf{F}_{1^{'}}$ and $709\mathrm{nm}$ for $^5{\mathrm{L}}_{6^{\prime}}\ \rightarrow\ ^{7}{\mathrm{F}}_{2^{\prime}}$ to overlapping transitions at 690.5 and $700\mathrm{nm}$ .[4a,c,16c,23] As such, shifting in the energy levels can lead to significant ambiguity with regard to the assignment of observed luminescent bands. In an effort to find equivalent emission signatures to inform the effects of structural features (e.g. coordination environment and geometry) on the luminescent emission, a proposed, semi-quantitative energy level diagram is provided (Figure S17), comparing the observed energy levels of 1, estimated via collected luminescence spectra (Figure S14 and S15), to other $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ containing materials (e.g. $\mathbf{AmCl}_{3}$ and $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}{:}\mathrm{ThO}_{2}\rangle$ .[24] A computational effort to validate these assignments is in progress and part of a larger effort to probe energy transfer in these and other related materials.
Notably, no $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ emission is observed when the crystal was excited at or around $504\mathrm{nm}$ , a common wavelength for direct excitation of the ${}^{5}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ energy level for $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ .[4c,f, 16c, 25] Moreover, no peak in this region was observed in the excitation spectrum when measured at $683\mathrm{nm}$ (the most intense peak in the spectrum). Interestingly, emission is observed (at $683\mathrm{nm}$ ) when excited at $455\mathrm{nm}$ ; a notable band in the corresponding excitation spectrum (Figure S14 in the SI). Efforts to collect a luminescent lifetime of 1 with current instrumentation were unsuccessful due to an incredibly short lifetime $(<10~\mathrm{{ns})}$ , perhaps owing to a quenching mechanism provided by the water molecule in the inner coordination sphere of the metal center.[26] Such quenching, paired with inefficient energy transfer of the formally Laporte forbidden 5f–5f transitions within the $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ , may may explain the lack of emission in 1 despite the ${}^{5}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ energy level ( $\approx504~\mathrm{{nm}}$ being a commonly utilized excited state to probe $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ electronic structure. Reconciling this observation is part of our computational effort as well.
This study represents the first use of a transplutonium element in the formation of a MOF, as well as the demonstration of the utility of MOFs as a platform for study thereof. The insertion of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ into the relatively stable GWMOF-6 architecture allows for probing of spectroscopic characteristics of the actinide metal center. Additional efforts are planned to determine any effects of concentration quenching, common in lanthanide materials, as well as to explore the possibility of 4f–5f energy transfer pathways by doping $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}^{3+}$ into other $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ -containing GWMOF-6 variants.
